"Welcome to the new and enhanced UN Comtrade (https://comtrade.un.org/licenseagreement.html)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://comtrade.un.org/licenseagreement.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 65\nL0:   * cite0†Image: United Nations cite1†United Nations†www.un.org L1:   * cite2†Department Of Economic and Social Affairs†www.un.org L2:   * cite3†Statistics Division†unstats.un.org L3:   * cite4†Trade Statistics†unstats.un.org L4: 07 August, 2012\nL5: UNITED NATIONS ON-LINE USAGE AGREEMENT PLEASE TAKE NOTICE -- PLEASE READ THIS FORM CAREFULLY By subscribing to the United Nations COMTRADE or using UN Comtrade without subscription, you are agreeing to abide by all of the following terms and conditions for your use of United Nations COMTRADE, in addition to and without derogating from the standard Terms of Use for United Nations Web Sites (available at cite5†http://www.un.org/en/aboutun/terms/†www.un.org ): 1. Definitions.\nL6: For the purposes of this Usage Agreement \"United Nations COMTRADE\" means the information service and content provided by United Nations Publications and the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs, by affiliates of United Nations Publications and by third parties; \"Premium Access Service\" means the information service which is accessible solely through an authorized account (\"Usage Account\" or \"Subscription\"); \"you\", \"your\" or \"yours\" refers to each person who, or each entity that, establishes a Usage Account or Subscription (the \"Subscriber\") or is authorized to access and use United Nations COMTRADE or uses United Nations COMTRADE without subscription.\nL7: Where any entity has established an \"institutional\" Usage Account with United Nations Publications for the use of the United Nations COMTRADE, the terms \"you\" or \"yours\" shall refer to such entity (including all locations, branches and facilities operated by such entity), any affiliates of such entity effectively controlled by such entity, and all authorized users of such entity, or of its affiliates, having access to use of the United Nations COMTRADE pursuant to such \"institutional\" Usage Account; \"Party\", or collectively \"Parties\", refers to the United Nations, including the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs and United Nations Publications, and each person who is authorized to access and use United Nations COMTRADE or uses United Nations COMTRADE without subscription.\nL8: 2. General. This Usage Agreement sets forth the terms and conditions of a license for use of the United Nations COMTRADE. By using United Nations COMTRADE, you consent and agree to comply with all of the terms and conditions of this Usage Agreement. The rights and obligations concerning the use of United Nations COMTRADE in accordance with this Usage Agreement are not transferable.\nL9: If you are a Subscriber you are responsible for any use of your Usage Account under your user ID (username) or password by any person or entity. You are further responsible for ensuring that any use of your Usage Account complies fully with the provisions of this Usage Agreement. You are responsible for protecting the confidentiality of your user ID and your password. 3. Changed Terms.\nL10: The United Nations shall have the sole and exclusive right at any time to alter or amend this Usage Agreement or to otherwise change, modify or amend the terms and conditions applicable to use of United Nations COMTRADE.\nL11: The United Nations shall have the sole and exclusive right to change or discontinue any aspect or feature of United Nations COMTRADE, including but not limited to content, functionality, hours of availability, and hardware or telecommunications equipment, software or services needed for access or use. The United Nations COMTRADE shall have no obligation to take the needs of any user into consideration in connection therewith.\nL12: The United Nations will provide reasonable notice to you of any changes, modifications or amendments to this Usage Agreement; however, any use of United Nations COMTRADE by you after any such changes, modifications, or additions shall be governed by this Usage Agreement. 4. Equipment.\nL13: You are solely responsible for obtaining, maintaining and all costs associated with any telephone, computer hardware and software, modems, telecommunications connections, and/or other equipment and/or services which are or may be needed for access to and use of United Nations COMTRADE. 5. Usage. United Nations COMTRADE contains copyrighted material and/or other proprietary information.\nL14: The materials contained on or otherwise made available to you on United Nations COMTRADE may be copyrighted by the United Nations and, thus, are protected by copyright laws and regulations worldwide.\nL15: Except as may otherwise be expressly permitted by applicable law, any copying, automated browsing or downloading, redistribution, publication, or commercial exploitation of any material contained on or otherwise made available to you on United Nations COMTRADE is strictly prohibited without the prior written permission of the United Nations.\nL16: Any requests for permission to reproduce or otherwise use any material contained on or otherwise made available to you on United Nations COMTRADE should be directed to The Director of the United Nations Statistics Division: comtrade@un.org Without limiting, derogating from or otherwise affecting the validity of the foregoing, you may not alter or remove any copyright symbol or other identification or any information concerning authorship of any of the materials contained on or otherwise made available to you on United Nations COMTRADE.\nL17: The provisions of this Usage Agreement shall inure to the benefit of the United Nations, including United Nations Publications, its affiliates, and its third-party content providers and licensors. 6. Disclaimer of Warranty; Limitation of Liability. YOU EXPRESSLY ACKNOWLEDGE AND AGREE THAT ANY AND ALL USE OF UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE IS AT YOUR SOLE RISK.\nL18: UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\" WITHOUT ANY WARRANTIES OF ANY KIND, EITHER EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO WARRANTIES OF TITLE OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NON-INFRINGEMENT.\nL19: NEITHER THE UNITED NATIONS, INCLUDING THE UNITED NATIONS DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL AFFAIRS AND UNITED NATIONS PUBLICATIONS, NOR ITS AFFILIATES, OR ANY OF ITS EMPLOYEES, AGENTS, THIRD-PARTY CONTENT PROVIDERS, OR LICENSORS WARRANT THAT ACCESS TO AND USE OF UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE WILL BE FREE FROM INTERRUPTIONS OR FREE FROM ERRORS OR DEFECTS; NOR DOES THE UNITED NATIONS MAKE ANY WARRANTY AS TO THE RESULTS THAT MAY BE OBTAINED FROM USE OF UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE, OR AS TO THE ACCURACY, RELIABILITY, COMPLETENESS OR CONTENT OF ANY INFORMATION OR SERVICE PROVIDED THROUGH UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE.\nL20: YOU FURTHER EXPRESSLY ACKNOWLEDGE AND AGREE THAT INFORMATION, TEXT, GRAPHICS, AND HYPERLINKS PROVIDED TO YOU THROUGH UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE AND LOCATED ON OTHER SITES THROUGHOUT THE COMBINED GLOBAL ELECTRONIC NETWORKS KNOWN AS THE INTERNET AND THE WORLD-WIDE-WEB ARE PROVIDED SOLELY AS A RESOURCE AND A CONVENIENCE TO YOU. SUCH HYPERLINKS TO OTHER SITES ARE NOT UNDER THE CONTROL OF THE UNITED NATIONS NOR DO THEY REPRESENT AN ENDORSEMENT BY THE UNITED NATIONS OF THOSE SITES.\nL21: THE UNITED NATIONS MAKES NO WARRANTY, EITHER EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, AS TO THE ACCURACY, RELIABILITY, OR CONTENT OF SUCH INFORMATION, TEXT, GRAPHICS, AND HYPERLINKS. THE UNITED NATIONS DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL AFFAIRS AND UNITED NATIONS PUBLICATIONS HAVE NOT TESTED ANY SOFTWARE LOCATED ON OTHER SITES AND DO NOT MAKE ANY REPRESENTATIONS AS TO THE QUALITY, SAFETY, RELIABILITY OR SUITABILITY OF SUCH SOFTWARE.\nL22: IN NO EVENT SHALL THE UNITED NATIONS, INCLUDING THE UNITED NATIONS DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL AFFAIRS AND UNITED NATIONS PUBLICATIONS, BE LIABLE TO YOU OR ANY OTHER PERSON FOR ANY LOSS OF BUSINESS OR PROFITS, OR FOR ANY DIRECT, INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL OR CONSEQUENTIAL DAMAGES ARISING OUT OF ANY USE OF, OR INABILITY TO USE, UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE, INCLUDING, WITHOUT LIMITATION, ANY FAULT, ERROR, OMISSION, INTERRUPTION OR DELAY WITH RESPECT THERETO, EVEN IF THE UNITED NATIONS, INCLUDING THE UNITED NATIONS DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL AFFAIRS AND UNITED NATIONS PUBLICATIONS, WERE PREVIOUSLY ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGES, OR FOR ANY OTHER CLAIM BY YOU OR ANY OTHER PERSON.\nL23: YOU SPECIFICALLY ACKNOWLEDGE AND AGREE THAT THE UNITED NATIONS IS NOT LIABLE FOR ANY CONDUCT OF ANY USER. YOU ACKNOWLEDGE AND AGREE THAT THE DELIVERY SYSTEM USED FOR UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE, I.E., THE INTERNET AND THE WORLD-WIDE-WEB, ARE UNPREDICTABLE AND MAY, FROM TIME TO TIME, IMPEDE ACCESS TO UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE.\nL24: NEITHER THE UNITED NATIONS, INCLUDING THE UNITED NATIONS DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL AFFAIRS AND UNITED NATIONS PUBLICATIONS, NOR ITS AFFILIATES, OR ANY OF ITS EMPLOYEES, AGENTS, THIRD-PARTY CONTENT PROVIDERS, OR LICENSORS SHALL BE LIABLE FOR ANY FAILURE OF PERFORMANCE, COMPUTER VIRUS, COMMUNICATION FAILURE, OR ANY OTHER INTERFERENCE WITH YOUR USE OF OR ACCESS TO UNITED NATIONS COMTRADE, REGARDLESS OF CAUSE, OR FOR ANY DAMAGES RESULTING THEREFROM. 7. Indemnification.\nL25: As a condition of use of United Nations COMTRADE, you agree to indemnify the United Nations and/or its affiliates from and against any and all actions, claims, losses, damages, liabilities and expenses (including reasonable attorney's fees) by your institute arising out of your use of United Nations COMTRADE, including, without limitation, any claims alleging facts that if true would constitute a breach by you of this Usage Agreement.\nL26: If you are dissatisfied with any material published by United Nations COMTRADE, your sole and exclusive remedy is to discontinue using United Nations COMTRADE. 8. Termination. The United Nations, including the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs and United Nations Publications, may terminate this Usage Agreement at any time by providing reasonable prior notice to you.\nL27: Without derogating from or otherwise limiting the foregoing, the United Nations, including the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs and United Nations Publications, shall have the right to immediately terminate this Usage Agreement and/or your rights to use United Nations COMTRADE in the event of any breach by you of this Usage Agreement.\nL28: You may terminate this Usage Agreement by providing written notice of termination to the United Nations Publications within 15 days from the date of the activation of your United Nations COMTRADE Usage Account. Termination of this Agreement automatically terminates your license to use the United Nations COMTRADE, including, as applicable, the Premium Access Service, and any content or other material contained therein.\nL29: In the event of a termination of this Usage Agreement by the United Nations, including the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs and United Nations Publications, other than as a result of your breach, United Nations Publications shall refund to you any pro rata portion of the subscription fee paid by you to the United Nations Publications, if any, for the unexpired portion of the term of this Usage Agreement. 9.\nL30: Settlement of disputes Amicable settlement: The Parties shall use their best efforts to amicably settle any dispute, controversy, or claim arising out of this Usage Agreement or the breach, termination, or invalidity thereof.\nL31: Where the Parties wish to seek such an amicable settlement through conciliation, the conciliation shall take place in accordance with the Conciliation Rules then obtaining of the United Nations Commission on International Trade Law (\"UNCITRAL\"), or according to such other procedure as may be agreed between the Parties in writing.\nL32: Arbitration: Any dispute, controversy, or claim between the Parties arising out of this Usage Agreement or the breach, termination, or invalidity thereof, unless settled amicably under the preceding paragraph, within sixty (60) days after receipt by one Party of the other Party�s written request for such amicable settlement, shall be referred by either Party to arbitration in accordance with the UNCITRAL Arbitration Rules then obtaining.\nL33: The decisions of the arbitral tribunal shall be based on general principles of international commercial law.\nL34: The arbitral tribunal shall be empowered to order the return or destruction of goods or any property, whether tangible or intangible, or of any confidential information provided under this Usage Agreement, order the termination of the Usage Agreement, or order that any other protective measures be taken with respect to the goods, services or any other property, whether tangible or intangible, or of any confidential information provided under this Usage Agreement, as appropriate, all in accordance with the authority of the arbitral tribunal pursuant to Article 26 (\"Interim measures\") and Article 34 (\"Form and effect of the award\") of the UNCITRAL Arbitration Rules.\nL35: The arbitral tribunal shall have no authority to award punitive damages. In addition, unless otherwise expressly provided in this Usage Agreement, the arbitral tribunal shall have no authority to award interest in excess of the London Inter-Bank Offered Rate (\"LIBOR\") then prevailing, and any such interest shall be simple interest only. The Parties shall be bound by any arbitration award rendered as a result of such arbitration as the final adjudication of any such dispute, controversy, or claim. 10.\nL36: Preservation of Immunities. Nothing contained in or related to this Usage Agreement shall be deemed a waiver, express or implied, of the privileges and immunities of the United Nations, including its subsidiary organs, which are specifically reserved. 11. Miscellaneous. The United Nations reserves the right to deny in its sole discretion any user access to this Site or any portion thereof without notice.\nL37: No waiver by the United Nations, including the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs and United Nations Publications, of any provisions of the terms and conditions applicable to use United Nations COMTRADE shall be binding except as set forth in writing and signed by their duly authorized representative.\nL38: This Usage Agreement, the standard Terms of Use for United Nations Web Sites, and any United Nations policies, including those of the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs and United Nations Publications, relating to your operation of United Nations COMTRADE constitute the entire agreement and understanding between the United Nations and you with respect to the subject matter hereof, and supersede all previous written or oral agreements with respect to such subject matter.\nL39: No waiver by any party hereto of any of the provisions hereof or of any breach or other default hereunder shall be deemed to be a waiver of any preceding or subsequent breach or default. The section headings used herein are for convenience only and shall not be given any legal import. The provisions of Sections 5, 6, 7, 9, 10 and 11 shall survive any termination of this Usage Agreement.\nL40: ###### My API Portal\nL41: \nL42:   * cite6†API Explorer†comtradeapi.un.org L43: \nL44: ###### Premium Services\nL45: \nL46:   * cite7†View Premium Packages†shop.un.org L47: \nL48: ###### Jump To\nL49: \nL50:   * cite4†Trade Statistics Section†unstats.un.org L51:   * cite8†United Nations Statistics Division†unstats.un.org L52:   * cite9†Comtrade @ UN Data†data.un.org L53: \nL54: ###### Help & Support\nL55: \nL56:   * Mail to comtrade@un.org\nL57:   * cite10†Wiki†unstats.un.org L58: \nL59: * * *\nL60: \nL61: Copyrights � United Nations 2022.\nL62: \nL63:   * cite11†Terms of Use†www.un.org L64:   * cite12†License Agreement --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://comtrade.un.org/data/MethodologyGuideforComtradePlus.pdf)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://comtrade.un.org/data/MethodologyGuideforComtradePlus.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 488\nL0@P0: 1\nL1@P0: Methodology Guide for UN Comtrade User on\nL2@P0: UN Comtrade Upgrade 2019\nL3@P0: Contents\nL4@P0: I. Background .........................................................................................................................................2\nL5@P0: Table 1: Overview of Major Improvements Resulting from UN Comtrade Processing System .....2\nL6@P0: II. Integration of All Data Processing & Dissemination...................................................................3\nL7@P0: III. New Data Items...............................................................................................................................3\nL8@P0: A. Mode of transport ...........................................................................................................................3\nL9@P0: B. Customs procedure codes...............................................................................................................4\nL10@P0: C. Second partner ................................................................................................................................5\nL11@P0: D. FOB Valuation for Imports............................................................................................................6\nL12@P0: E. Expanded breakdown of types of trade flows...............................................................................6\nL13@P0: F. Alternative quantity units, in addition to net weight and WCO recommended units..............7\nL14@P0: G. Gross and net weight ................................................................................................................10\nL15@P0: IV. Improved Quantity Estimation for Commodities......................................................................10\nL16@P0: V. Pre-aggregated data..........................................................................................................................10\nL17@P0: VI. Improved Conversions between Classifications.........................................................................11\nL18@P0: A. Conversions for commodities.......................................................................................................11\nL19@P0: B. Conversions for services...............................................................................................................11\nL20@P0-1: Annex 1. Quantity Estimation .................................................................................................................132\nL21@P1: I. Background\nL22@P1: Beginning in 2018, UN Comtrade has upgraded its trade data processing system, which will result in\nL23@P1: improved features and functionality of the UN Comtrade database. The new system, named UN Comtrade\nL24@P1: Processing System, allows for processing and disseminating several new data items as recommended in the\nL25@P1: International Merchandise Trade Statistics: Concepts and Definitions 2010 (IMTS2010); improves the\nL26@P1: quantity estimation for IMTS; expands the conversion of commodities at more detailed HS levels; and\nL27@P1: allows for the dissemination of statistics of trade in services (SITS) in both Extended Balance of Payments\nL28@P1: (EBOPS) 2002 and EBOPS2010 classifications. This User Guide is intended for users of Comtrade to better\nL29@P1: understand the changes and the new data items now available. Table 1 presents a summary of the major\nL30@P1: changes and the impact on Comtrade data, followed by more detailed technical notes.\nL31@P1: Table 1: Overview of Major Improvements Resulting from UN Comtrade Processing System\nL32@P1: Previous System Upgrades made with new UN Comtrade\nL33@P1: Processing System\nL34@P1: Result for Comtrade Users\nL35@P1: Three separate processing\nL36@P1: systems for annual and\nL37@P1: monthly IMTS and SITS\nL38@P1: One integrated processing system for all trade\nL39@P1: data\nL40@P1: Improved consistency and timeliness of\nL41@P1: dissemination of trade data. Monthly\nL42@P1: IMTS will be disseminated the same way\nL43@P1: as annual IMTS data (e.g., will be\nL44@P1: converted to other classifications and\nL45@P1: missing quantities will be estimated)\nL46@P1: Data items for IMTS included\nL47@P1: trade by commodity and by\nL48@P1: partner\nL49@P1: In addition, new data items now include (when\nL50@P1: reported):\nL51@P1: • mode of transport\nL52@P1: • customs procedure codes\nL53@P1: • 2\nL54@P1: nd partner\nL55@P1: More information on the nature of trade\nL56@P1: flows and partner country attribution,\nL57@P1: allowing for better analysis of bilateral\nL58@P1: trade asymmetries\nL59@P1: Single valuation for IMTS\nL60@P1: imports (at CIF) and exports\nL61@P1: (at FOB)\nL62@P1: In addition, FOB valuation for imports (when\nL63@P1: reported)\nL64@P1: Symmetrical valuation of imports and\nL65@P1: exports and more information on\nL66@P1: insurance and freight costs, allowing for\nL67@P1: better analysis of bilateral trade\nL68@P1: asymmetries\nL69@P1: Trade flows for IMTS\nL70@P1: consisted of Total Exports:\nL71@P1: Re-exports; Total Imports; and\nL72@P1: Re-Imports\nL73@P1: Expanded breakdown of trade flows for IMTS\nL74@P1: (when reported) including Domestic Exports,\nL75@P1: Export/Import of goods after/for inward\nL76@P1: processing; Export/Import of goods for/after\nL77@P1: outward processing; and Export/Import on intra\u0002firm trade\nL78@P1: More information on the nature of trade\nL79@P1: flows, especially re-exports, re-imports,\nL80@P1: and goods for processing, and intra-firm\nL81@P1: trade\nL82@P1: IMTS quantity units\nL83@P1: standardised to 12 World\nL84@P1: Customs Organization (WCO)\nL85@P1: recommended units\nL86@P1: In addition, standardization to more quantity\nL87@P1: units (43+ units of which twelve as WCO\nL88@P1: recommended units), when reported. These\nL89@P1: quantity units comply with trade SDMX code\nL90@P1: list.\nL91@P1: Additional measures of quantity\nL92@P1: Single weight (net weight in\nL93@P1: kg) for IMTS\nL94@P1: In addition, gross weight (net weight plus the\nL95@P1: weight of the shipping or cargo container), when\nL96@P1: reported\nL97@P1: More information on weight and shipping\nL98@P1: Limited quantity estimations\nL99@P1: for IMTS (not all commodities\nL100@P1: estimated)\nL101@P1: Estimation of quantity for all commodities;\nL102@P1: when applicable. However, some may not be\nL103@P1: shown for specific commodities\nL104@P1: More complete quantity data; especially\nL105@P1: for aggregated data\nL106@P1: Conversion for IMTS at 6-\nL107@P1: digit HS level\nL108@P1: Conversion at any HS level and improved\nL109@P1: conversion of residuals\nL110@P1: More complete converted datasets\nL111@P1: Only EBOPS2002 data\nL112@P1: disseminated for SITS\nL113@P1: (EBOPS2010 data converted\nL114@P1: to EBOPS2002)\nL115@P1: SITS data also disseminated in EBOPS2010\nL116@P1: (when reported in EBOPS2010)\nL117@P1: SITS data in both EBOPS2002 and\nL118@P1-2: EBOPS2010, when reported3\nL119@P2: II. Integration of All Data Processing & Dissemination\nL120@P2: The new processing system, UN Comtrade Processing System, integrates the processing and dissemination\nL121@P2: of all trade data: annual IMTS, monthly IMTS and annual SITS. Previously, there were three separate\nL122@P2: processing and dissemination systems. The integration will allow for more consistent and timely processing\nL123@P2: and dissemination of trade data on UN Comtrade.\nL124@P2: III. New Data Items\nL125@P2: UN Comtrade will now disseminate the following new data items for IMTS, when they are reported by the\nL126@P2: reporting country.\nL127@P2: Please note: The availability of data items can be consulted in metadata table.\nL128@P2: A. Mode of transport\nL129@P2: IMTS 2010 recommends that the mode of transport (MoT) should be recorded as the means of\nL130@P2: transport used when goods enter or leave the economic territory of a country. It further recommends\nL131@P2: that countries compile MoT at the most detailed commodity level. Not every country is reporting\nL132@P2: MoT to UN Comtrade.\nL133@P2: For those countries that are reporting MoT, the standardised international classification used for\nL134@P2: dissemination on UN Comtrade is as presented in IMTS2010 (see IMTS, para. 7.2):\nL135@P2: All modes of transport\nL136@P2: 1. Air\nL137@P2: 2. Water\nL138@P2:  2.1 Sea\nL139@P2:  2.2 Inland waterway\nL140@P2: 3. Land\nL141@P2:  3.1 Railway\nL142@P2:  3.2 Road\nL143@P2: 4. Not elsewhere classified\nL144@P2:  4.1 Pipelines and cables\nL145@P2:  4.1.1 Pipelines\nL146@P2:  4.1.2 Cables\nL147@P2:  4.2 Postal consignments, mail or courier shipments\nL148@P2:  4.3 Self-propelled goods\nL149@P2:  4.4 Other\nL150@P2: Depending on their national requirements countries may report mode of transport at the one-, two\u0002or three-digit level or create even more detailed breakdowns. When necessary, UNSD aligns the\nL151@P2: national reported MoT to the standardised classification above, based on feedback from the\nL152@P2: reporting country as needed.\nL153@P2: If no MoT data are reported for a particular record, the MoT value is marked as “not specified”\nL154@P2: (code 9000) in UN Comtrade, but that record will be included in the aggregated “All Modes of\nL155@P2-3: Transport”.4\nL156@P3: B. Customs procedure codes\nL157@P3: Customs authorities usually apply a system of codes that allows flows of goods to be identified for\nL158@P3: inclusion or exclusion under general or special trade and prevents double recording of the goods\nL159@P3: which have undergone several customs procedures. While there is a standardised code list of\nL160@P3: customs procedure codes, which is based on the Revised Kyoto Convention (RKC) of World\nL161@P3: Customs Organisation (see IMTS 2010, Annex B and IMTS, 2010 Chapter VII, para. 8.5), in\nL162@P3: practice, countries do not strictly follow the standards of the RKC. Some countries apply their own\nL163@P3: unique national customs procedure codes.\nL164@P3: UNSD maps national customs procedure codes to the standard RKC codes for dissemination on\nL165@P3: UN Comtrade, based on the code description provided in the metadata and communication with\nL166@P3: the reporting country. It is worth noting that certain codes in the RKC code list are intended only\nL167@P3: for exports or imports, or to be excluded from IMTS, according to IMTS2010 recommendations.\nL168@P3: UNSD makes efforts to consult with reporting countries to ensure that UN Comtrade excludes\nL169@P3: records associated with customs procedure codes that are to be excluded from IMTS.\nL170@P3: UN Comtrade’s standard list of customs procedure codes and associated trade flows, based on the\nL171@P3: RKC, is presented in table 2 below.\nL172@P3-4: Table 2: UNSD Customs Procedure Codes5\nL173@P4: Note: In certain cases where a nationally reported customs procedure code identifies a specific trade\nL174@P4: flow (notably flows to/from processing zones and/or identification of intra-firm trade) and the\nL175@P4: country did not make a symmetrical recording in its trade flow data (see section III.B.4 below),\nL176@P4: UNSD will adjust the trade flow to align with the flow indicated by the customs procedure code.\nL177@P4: C. Second partner\nL178@P4: IMTS 2010 recommends for partner attribution that (a) in the case of imports, the country of\nL179@P4: origin1should be recorded; and (b) in the case of exports, the country of last known destination\nL180@P4: should be recorded.\nL181@P4: IMTS2010 also recommends that country of consignment be recorded for imports as the second\nL182@P4: partner country attribution, alongside country of origin, and encourages that country of\nL183@P4: consignment be recorded for exports as the second partner country attribution.2 Accordingly, a data\nL184@P4: field for “2nd partner” is now included on UN Comtrade.\nL185@P4: The country of consignment in the case of imports is the country from which goods were dispatched\nL186@P4: to the importing country, without any commercial transactions or other operations that change the\nL187@P4: legal status of the goods taking place in any intermediate country. If, before arriving in the\nL188@P4: importing country, goods enter one or more further countries and are subject to such transactions\nL189@P4: or operations, that last intermediate country where such transactions or operations took place should\nL190@P4: be taken as the country of consignment.3 In the case of exports, the country of consignment is\nL191@P4: defined in a symmetrical way; i.e., it is the country to which goods are dispatched by the exporting\nL192@P4: country, without—as far as it is known at the time of exportation—being subject to any commercial\nL193@P4: transactions or other operations that change the legal status of the goods taking place in any\nL194@P4: intermediate country. If there are several intermediate countries, then the first intermediate country\nL195@P4: after leaving the exporting country should be recorded as country of consignment or destination.4\nL196@P4: This additional detail will aid in the analysis of bilateral trade asymmetries, as imports on the basis\nL197@P4: of country of consignment is a more symmetrical recording of the mirror exports. In other words,\nL198@P4: the bilateral comparison of country of origin at imports with country of final destination at exports\nL199@P4: is not a symmetrical recording of the same trade transactions by the exporting country and the\nL200@P4: importing country. To be more precise, the current identification of the trading partner (for imports)\nL201@P4: by country of origin may “skip” countries in which value added does not reach the level recognized\nL202@P4: as substantial transformation, while country of final destination would generally be the next country\nL203@P4: \nL204@P4: 1 Country of origin means the country in which the goods have been produced or manufactured, according to the\nL205@P4: criteria laid down for the purposes of application of the Customs tariff. Two basic criteria determine origin: (a) the\nL206@P4: criterion of goods “wholly produced (obtained)” in a given country, where only one country enters into consideration\nL207@P4: in attributing origin and (b) the criterion of “substantial transformation”.\nL208@P4: 2\nL209@P4: considering that, in the case of exports, countries often do not differentiate the country of last known destination and\nL210@P4: the country of consignment and that their separate recording could create a significant additional data-reporting and\nL211@P4: data-processing burden, the compilation of export statistics on the country of consignment basis is only encouraged,\nL212@P4: depending on a country’s needs and circumstances. It is recognized that the compilation of country of consignment\nL213@P4: for exports may be considered by some countries as a longer-term objective.\nL214@P4: 3 Please note that, for imports, country of origin and country of consignment can be the same if no commercial\nL215@P4: transactions or other operations that change the legal status of the goods took place in any intermediate country.\nL216@P4: 4 Please note that, for exports, country of last known destination and country of consignment can be the same if no\nL217@P4: commercial transactions or other operations that change the legal status of the goods took place in any intermediate\nL218@P4-5: country.6\nL219@P5: where some transformation takes place. This creates incomparability and detracts from the\nL220@P5: usefulness of such trade data for some types of economic analysis. Alternatively, compiling trade\nL221@P5: data by the country of consignment offers the possibility of obtaining consistent statistics and\nL222@P5: reasonable comparability since it promotes the recording of the same transactions by importing and\nL223@P5: exporting countries. This approach should result in more symmetrical data since goods recorded as\nL224@P5: imports by one country are to be recorded as exports by another.\nL225@P5: Box 1. Implementation of two partner countries in UN Comtrade Data Interface\nL226@P5: At the most basic data record, up two partner countries would be according to the following\nL227@P5: rules:\nL228@P5: a) When only one partner country is reported; then it would be allocated as “partner 1”.\nL229@P5: The partner attribution can be both origin/consignment for imports; and this\nL230@P5: information can be consulted in metadata table\nL231@P5: b) When two partner countries are reported; then “country of origin for imports” will be\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.census.gov/foreign-trade/reference/guides/Guide_to_International_Trade_Datasets.pdf)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.census.gov/foreign-trade/reference/guides/Guide_to_International_Trade_Datasets.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1023\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCensus Data API User Guide (https://www.census.gov/data/developers/guidance/api-user-guide.Query_Limits.html)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.census.gov/data/developers/guidance/api-user-guide.Query_Limits.html\",\"lineno\":n